<section class="grid gap-6 xl:grid-cols-3">
  <div class="space-y-6 xl:col-span-2">
    <article class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <div>
          <h2 class="card-title">What this project does</h2>
          <p class="card-subtitle">A solo personal finance tracker for the H6WAD final project</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-sm max-w-none space-y-3 text-slate-600">
        <p>
          Finance Tracker stores accounts, categories, transactions and monthly budgets in a SQLite
          database. Every page is rendered on the server with Express and EJS, and the dashboard and
          budget pages add client-side visualisations with Chart.js.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app demonstrates full CRUD behaviour: you can create, read, update and delete
          transactions, accounts and categories, and create or replace budgets for any month. All
          money is stored as integer cents so totals never suffer from floating point rounding.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <div>
          <h2 class="card-title">How the data is modelled</h2>
          <p class="card-subtitle">Four related tables with constraints and triggers</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">accounts</h3>
          <p class="mt-1">Name, type (current, savings, credit, cash), opening balance and a colour used in the interface.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">categories</h3>
          <p class="mt-1">Name, kind (income or expense) and a colour. Names are unique regardless of case.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">transactions</h3>
          <p class="mt-1">Links one account and one category, with a type, positive amount, description and date. Foreign keys use <code>ON DELETE RESTRICT</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">budgets</h3>
          <p class="mt-1">A planned amount per category and month. A unique constraint on the pair makes saving an upsert.</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Two triggers reject any transaction whose type does not match the kind of its category, so
          income can never be filed under an expense category even if a request bypasses the form.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <div>
          <h2 class="card-title">Validation and security</h2>
          <p class="card-subtitle">Checks on the client, on the server and in the database</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
        <li>HTML inputs use types, lengths and limits to guide the user.</li>
        <li>The server re-validates every field: amounts, dates, descriptions, foreign keys and colours.</li>
        <li>SQLite CHECK constraints, foreign keys and triggers enforce the same rules at the storage layer.</li>
        <li>EJS escapes output by default, so user text cannot inject HTML.</li>
        <li>Helmet sets security headers and the session cookie is HTTP-only and same-site.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>

